This paper investigates the existence of standardized ground states for the given biharmonic Choquard type equation \begin{align*}
\begin{split}
\left\{
\begin{array}{ll}
\Delta^2u-\beta\Delta u=\lambda u+\left(\frac{1}{|x|^\mu}*F(u)\right)f(u),
\quad\mbox{in}\ \ \mathbb{R}^4,
\displaystyle\int_{\mathbb{R}^4}|u|^2dx=c^2,\quad u\in H^2(\mathbb{R}^4),
\end{array}
\end{split}
\end{align*} under specific constraints and growth conditions for the functions $f(u)$ and $F(u)$. We establish the standardized ground state scenario through a homotopy-based minimax principle and variational approach, leveraging the compactness of Palais-Smale sequences, inspired by the recent works on the Choquard equation. Here, the critical exponent is found to be $\mathbb{R}^4$, and the indicated integration and gradient terms exhibit critical growth with respect to $u$. Additionally, we demonstrate the occurrence of a non-radial solution in this context. The given analysis provides an outcome of which we take advantage to come up with a related multiplicity of solutions for the given biharmonic Choquard type equation. Finally, the declared result regarding the existence of standardized ground states remains valid under an alternative set of growth conditions specified in the paper. This research is an expansion of our earlier work established in our paper published under the title 'Ground states for a biharmonic Choquard type equation with exponential critical growth' published in the journal Nonlinear Analysis: Real Variable, Volume 3, Issue 11, 2020, 100015. Here, we relax the specified growth conditions on the function $f(u)$ and compensate with additional constraints on the function $F(u)$. This research is an expansion of our earlier work established in our paper published under the title 'Ground states for a biharmonic Choquard type equation with exponential critical growth' published in the journal Nonlinear Analysis: Real Variable, Volume 3, Issue 11, 2020, 100015. Here, we relax the specified growth conditions on the function $f(u)$ and compensate with additional constraints on the function $F(u)$. This research is an expansion of our earlier work established in our

